WEBVTT

00:00:00.556 --> 00:00:03.066
A cipher is only as strong as its key.

00:00:03.591 --> 00:00:05.261
If the key space is small,

00:00:05.671 --> 00:00:07.281
an attacker tries them all.

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:11.839
Key space is the set of all possible keys.

00:00:12.459 --> 00:00:16.609
For a 70-bit key,
that is about 10 to the 21 keys.

00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:23.074
Kahate's rule of thumb: 70 bits needed
for a key space of 10 to the 21.

00:00:24.889 --> 00:00:26.579
Each bit doubles the work.

00:00:27.094 --> 00:00:31.924
A k-bit key costs about c times
2 to the k minus 1 to exhaust.

00:00:32.589 --> 00:00:36.339
Increase the key by one bit,
and the attacker's work doubles.

00:00:38.056 --> 00:00:39.906
Secure key sizes today.

00:00:40.441 --> 00:00:41.591
Symmetric keys:

00:00:42.021 --> 00:00:43.891
128 bits or more.

00:00:44.361 --> 00:00:45.591
RSA keys:

00:00:46.181 --> 00:00:51.011
2048 bits or more,
because of modular exponentiation attacks.

00:00:51.621 --> 00:00:53.971
Comparable security, different cost.

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:57.512
Brute force on a 4-bit key:

00:00:58.182 --> 00:00:58.852
16 tries.

00:00:59.512 --> 00:01:02.802
On an 8-bit key: 256 tries.

00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:05.752
On a 128-bit key:

00:01:06.322 --> 00:01:08.472
more tries than atoms in the universe.

00:01:09.127 --> 00:01:10.737
Key size is the difference.

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:16.199
Large key space means
brute force is infeasible.

00:01:16.614 --> 00:01:19.244
That is why key size is the first defense.

